{
  "id": "bible-in-a-year",
  "title": "Bible in a Year",
  "description": "Every node of the 66-book core (the canon shared by all Christian traditions; deuterocanonical layers can be added alongside) in a year-long sequence that alternates Old and New Testament blocks — the OT in narrative-historical order with the prophets placed in their historical moments and psalms woven in beside the events they voice; the NT in canonical order alongside. Ordered for daily rhythm rather than by dependency: this is order: advisory on purpose. Interleaving the Gospels from January means the New Testament is regularly read ahead of the Old Testament passages it builds on, which is the trade a year-long plan makes so that no reader spends the first four months in Leviticus. Where a passage assumes something not yet read, the node's own prerequisite edges say so and a reader can follow them at any point.",
  "audience": "A reader committing to the whole Bible in roughly a year, at four to five nodes a week.",
  "coverage": "full",
  "order": "advisory",
  "canon_layers": [
    "core"
  ],
